y in inertial and noninertial frames and the principle of equivalence, Phys. Rev. D 84, 085018 (2011).[16] S. T. Pereira &amp; R. M. Angelo, Galilei covariance and Einstein's equivalence principle in quantum reference frames, Phys. Rev. A 91, 022107 (2015)[17] M. Zych &amp; Č. Brukner, Quantum formulation of the Einstein equivalence principle, Nature Phys. 14, 1027 (2018).[18] F. Giacomini, E. Castro-Ruiz &amp; Č. Brukner, Quantum mechanics and the covariance of physical laws in quantum reference frames, Nature Comm. 10, 494 (2019).[19] L. Hardy, arXiv:1903.01289, (2019).[20] S. Das, M. Fridman &amp; G. Lambiase, General formalism of the quantum equivalence principle, Comm. Phys. 6, 198 (2023).[21] C. Marletto &amp; V. Vedral, On the Testability of the Equivalence Principle as a Gauge Principle Detecting the Gravitational T^3 Phase, Front. Phys. 8, 176 (2020).[22] R. Howl, R. Penrose &amp; I. Fuentes, Exploring the unification of quantum theory and general relativity with a Bose-Einstein condensate, New Journal of Physics 21, 043047 (2019).[23] T. A. Wagner, S. Schlamminger, J. H. Gundlach, &amp; E. G. Adelberger, Torsion-balance tests of the weak equivalence principle, Classical Quantum Gravity 29, 184002 (2012).[24] P. Touboul et al., MICROSCOPE Mission: First Results of a Space Test of the Equivalence Principle, Phys. Rev. Lett. 119, 231101 (2017).[25] P. Asenbaum, C. Overstreet, M. Kim , J. Curti, &amp; M. A. Kasevich, Atom-Interferometric Test of the Equivalence Principle at the 10^{-12} Level, Phys. Rev. Lett. 125, 191101 (2020).[26] As an interesting contextual anecdote, one may say that the mathematical description of free fall, as discovered by Galileo, is governed by powers of t: the acceleration is proportional to t^0, the velocity to t^1, and the position to t^2. In this work we extend this framework and demonstrate experimentally that the quantum phase of a freely falling mass follows a t^3 power law.[27] This point may be found in numerous books and articles. For example, we refer the reader to The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, Space and Time: Inertial Frames, where it is written: "The equivalence principle suggests, however, that a freely-falling frame of reference is physically indistinguishable from an inertial frame". https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2015/entries/spacetime-inframes/. See also, H. C. Ohanian, What is the principle of equivalence?, Am. J. Phys. 45, 903 (1977).[28] C. G. Darwin, Free motion in the wave mechanics, Proc. R. Soc. Lond. A 117, 258 (1927).[29] E.H. Kennard, Zur Quantenmechanik einfacher Bewegungstypen, Zeischrift für Physik 44, 326 (1927).[30] E.H. Kennard, The quantum mechanics of an electron or other particle, Journal of the Franklin Institute 207, 47 (1929).
